WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.525
Detection rules evaluate security telemetry and generate signals when predefined conditions are met.

00:00:04.525 --> 00:00:08.610
They help transform raw events into information that analysts can investigate, correlate,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.311
and use during security operations.

00:00:10.311 --> 00:00:13.715
A detection is not the same thing as an alert.

00:00:13.715 --> 00:00:18.140
The detection is the logic and context that identifies a behavior of interest.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:23.586
An alert is one possible output that asks a human or process to review that signal.

00:00:23.586 --> 00:00:27.671
Good detections can also enrich cases, support hunting, or feed automated triage.

00:00:27.671 --> 00:00:30.734
The goal is not to create alerts for everything.

00:00:30.734 --> 00:00:33.798
More alert volume does not automatically create better security.

00:00:33.798 --> 00:00:37.882
A rule is useful when it points defenders toward activity that matters,

00:00:37.882 --> 00:00:42.307
gives enough context to support a decision, and fits the team's operating model.

00:00:42.307 --> 00:00:44.690
Effective detection engineering balances sensitivity, specificity, maintainability,

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:45.711
and analyst usability.

00:00:45.711 --> 00:00:49.796
A rule that is too broad can bury the SOC in noise,

00:00:49.796 --> 00:00:53.540
while a rule that is too narrow may miss important signals.
